00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.869 align:start position:0%
farmers in southern brazil are feeling  the economic brunt of the war in ukraine  three months after russia's invasion  they're still able to secure fertilizers  for their crops but at very high prices  brazil is the world's largest soy  producer and it's the only international  giant grain producer that is almost  totally dependent on imported  fertilizers 86 of what the country needs  comes from abroad and much of it from  russia and belarus  despite international sanctions against  moscow russian ships are still being  able to dock at brazilian ports  this is not enough for a market as big  as brazil's the war has complicated the  logistics of importing products from  that part of the world generating  insecurity among investors that's why  fertilizer prices have skyrocketed  last year each of these bags with one  ton of fertilizer used to cost six  hundred dollars now farmers are paying  twice as much for the same amount  brazil's president jaibo sonaru visited  moscow shortly before vladimir putin  ordered his troops to invade ukraine  he said latin america's largest economy  would be neutral in the war because  business with russia is fundamental  fertilizers are sacred for us  has the support of brazil's powerful  agricultural lobby even here in  prudentopolis where 75  of the population is of ukrainian  descent and he's running for re-election  in october  what's happening in ukraine is terrible  but brazil has to think about itself it  cannot get involved because 30 percent  of the fertilizers we import come from  russia and belarus  brazil has announced a three-decade plan  to reduce the country's dependence on  imported fertilizers  but experts say it could also take  decades before there are any results  meanwhile farmers in brazil are hoping  for the war to end  monica yanake of al jazeera  prudentopolis brazil
 

